<h2 style="margin-bottom: 14px;">After applying for SNAP</h2>

<dl class="ckeditor-accordion"><dt><b>What happens after I apply for SNAP?</b></dt>
	<dd>
	<p><strong>Once your application is received, OFI has 30 days to make a decision. We will reach out to you for more information by phone and by letter, if necessary:</strong></p>

	<p>All SNAP applicants are required to <strong>complete an interview</strong>. This can be done via phone or in person. An Eligibility Specialist will call to review your application information with you and make sure they have everything needed to make an eligibility decision.</p>

	<p>You will also be asked to <strong>provide proof </strong>of your identity, household income, household expenses, and other items depending on your circumstances within 10 days. It can speed up processing times if your documentation (like current pay stubs, rent receipts, childcare receipts, <span style="font-size:11pt"><span style="font-family:Aptos,sans-serif"><span style="font-family:&quot;Segoe Pro&quot;,sans-serif">utility </span></span></span>bills,etc.) is sent in along with your application.The items we need will be asked for in a letter and give you a date to submit information by. If you are having trouble providing the requested documents, contact us for help. </p>
	</dd>
	<dt><b>What happens after a decision is made?</b></dt>
	<dd>
	<p>We will send a letter called a <em>Notice of Decision</em> explaining why you were or were not approved within 30 days.</p>

	<ul><li>If approved, the letter will include your monthly SNAP benefit amount. Additionally, if you have never had an EBT card, you will receive one in the mail within 5-7 days of the decision.</li>
		<li>If denied, the reason will be on the letter. You may be able to provide further information to change the decision within 30 days of the denial. You may also request an administrative hearing within 30 days of the denial, which is explained at the end of the letter<em>.</em></li>
	</ul></dd>
	<dt>
	<p><b>What happens if I am found eligible and I want SNAP to continue?</b></p>
	</dt>
	<dd>
	<p>During your 12 month certification period, you must submit a six-month report if any member of your household has earned an income, or if any members are 18-59 years old do not have a disabling condition. This report is sent by us and needs to be completed for SNAP to continue. Your <em>Notice of Decision</em> letter will outline any other circumstances you must report during the certification period. </p>

	<p>SNAP benefit eligibility is reviewed every 12 months. To continue receiving benefits at the end of your 12 month certification period, you must complete a renewal form (similar to the application), provide proof of eligibility factors (like rent, electricity, heating costs, etc.), and complete an interview (with some exceptions).</p>
	</dd>
	<dd>
	<ul></ul></dd>
</dl><h2>SNAP Eligibility </h2>

<dl class="ckeditor-accordion"><dt>How is SNAP eligibility determined?</dt>
	<dd>
	<p>To determine eligibility, we look at your SNAP household. A household includes anyone you buy and cook most of your meals with. If you live with your spouse and/or your child(ren) under 22, they must be included in your household (even if you do not buy and cook meals with them).</p>

	<p>Your household must meet certain requirements to be eligible for SNAP and receive benefits.</p>

	<p>We also look at financial resources, assets, and certain expenses for your household. We count income from most sources including cash assistance, Social Security payments, unemployment insurance payments, and child support. When you apply, tell OFI about any money you earn or that is given to you.</p>
	</dd>
	<dt>I am not a US Citizen. Could I be eligible for SNAP?</dt>
	<dd>
	<p>Maine extends SNAP benefits to certain Noncitizens who do not qualify for federal SNAP.</p>

	<p>SNAP is not part of a public charge test. If you are not a U.S. citizen or eligible immigrant, it is safe for you to apply and get SNAP for an eligible family member (like a U.S. citizen child).</p>

	<p>The Department does not report to USCIS, CBP, or ICE when an applicant reports being undocumented or presents expired documents. We also do not need to verify Noncitizen parent's immigration status if SNAP is being requested for their child who was born in the U.S..</p>

	<p>Maine is required to follow confidentiality practices and OFI only shares information when legally required:</p>

	<ul><li>Under certain situations for sponsored Lawful Permanent Residents</li>
		<li>If staff have seen proof of deportation notice, and the deportation date has passed.</li>
	</ul></dd>
	<dt>I need to submit proof of my expenses. What can I send in?</dt>
	<dd>
	<p>There are several ways to provide proof of shelter, utility, and care expenses. These items can be uploaded to your <a href="www.mymaineconnection.gov">My Maine Connection</a> account, mailed in, dropped off, or emailed to OFI. The chart below shows what documents you can submit:</p>

	<table border="1" cellpadding="1" cellspacing="1" style="width: 615px;"><tbody><tr><td style="width: 208px;"><strong>Lease/Rent Expense</strong></td>
				<td style="width: 390px;">
				<p>• Copy of a lease agreement.<br />
				• Rent bill.<br />
				• Written or verbal statement from the landlord.<br />
				• Rent receipt signed by the landlord/property owner.<br />
				• Copy of the redeemed check showing rent payment.<br />
				• Copy or screenshot of bank or financial mobile app (<em>Ex:</em> Venmo) withdrawal identifying the landlord as the recipient.<br />
				• A document from an agency providing a rent subsidy with the amount you are responsible for.<br />
				• A written or verbal statement from a non-household member roommate with the amount you are responsible for.</p>
				</td>
			</tr><tr><td style="width: 208px;"><strong>Mortgage Expense</strong></td>
				<td style="width: 390px;">
				<p>• Initial loan contract. <br />
				• Mortgage statement or payment coupons.<br />
				• Letter from the lien holder.<br />
				• Receipt from the lien holder.<br />
				• Copy of the redeemed check showing the mortgage payment.<br />
				• Copy or screenshot of bank or financial mobile app (<em>Ex:</em> Venmo) withdrawal showing the lean holder as the recipient.</p>
				</td>
			</tr><tr><td style="width: 208px;"><strong>Property Tax Expense</strong></td>
				<td style="width: 390px;"><br />
				• A bill from the municipality (within the past 12 months).<br />
				• Letter from the municipality.<br />
				• Receipt from the municipality.<br />
				• Copy of the redeemed check showing property tax payment.<br />
				 </td>
			</tr><tr><td style="width: 208px;"><strong>Utilities Expense</strong></td>
				<td style="width: 390px;">
				<p>• Lease showing the client is responsible for the utilities.<br />
				• Written or verbal statement from the landlord that you are responsible for the utilities.<br />
				• A bill from the utility provider.<br />
				• Written or verbal statement from a non-household member sharing the utilities.<br />
				• Receipt from the utility provider.<br />
				• Copy of the redeemed check paid to the utility provider.</p>
				</td>
			</tr><tr><td style="width: 208px;"><strong>Child Support Expense</strong></td>
				<td style="width: 390px;">
				<p><br />
				• A court order (confirms that it is legal due only).<br />
				• Written or verbal statement from the recipient.<br />
				• Receipt signed by the recipient.<br />
				• Copy of the redeemed check to the recipient showing the child support payment.<br />
				• Copy or screenshot of bank or financial mobile app (<em>Ex.</em> Venmo) withdrawal identifying the recipient.</p>

				<p>Note: the child support must be both legally due and actually paid.</p>
				</td>
			</tr><tr><td style="width: 208px;"><strong>Dependent Care Expense</strong></td>
				<td style="width: 390px;">
				<p>• Copy of a contract.<br />
				• Written or verbal statement from the care provider.<br />
				• Receipt signed by the care provider.<br />
				• Copy of the redeemed check showing child or adult care.<br />
				• Copy or screenshot of bank or financial mobile app (<em>Ex</em>. Venmo) withdrawal identifying the care provider as the recipient.</p>
				</td>
			</tr><tr><td style="width: 208px;"><strong>Excess Medical Expense</strong></td>
				<td style="width: 390px;">
				<p>• Copy of insurance policy or bills showing the insurance premium amount you pay.<br />
				• Receipts for over-the-counter medications, glasses, hearing aids, or other devices and a written or verbal statement from a health practitioner.<br />
				• Bills or receipts for procedures, services, or prescriptions showing the amount you are responsible for after all insurances and discounts.<br />
				• Receipts for food and/or veterinary bills for your animal specifically trained to serve the needs of someone who is over age 60 or has a disabling condition.<br />
				• Receipts for actual expenses and/or mileage records for transportation and lodging necessary to secure your medical treatment.<br />
				• Copy or screenshot of a bank or financial mobile app (<em>Ex.</em> Venmo) withdrawal identifying the provider as the recipient.</p>
				</td>
			</tr></tbody></table></dd>
</dl><h2>How to use and protect SNAP EBT cards</h2>

<dl class="ckeditor-accordion"><dt>Can an EBT card be used outside of Maine?</dt>
	<dd>
	<p><strong>EBT cards currently have out of State restriction effective August 2025.</strong> To help protect against electronic theft, EBT transactions continue to be temporarily restricted to use in Maine, New Hampshire, and Vermont only. This restriction does not affect online grocery orders being delivered to addresses in Maine. If you need to use your EBT card outside of Maine, New Hampshire, or Vermont, please call the Office for Family Independence Monday through Friday from 7:00AM to 4:00PM at 1-855-797-4357<b>.</b></p>
	</dd>
	<dt>What can SNAP benefits buy?</dt>
	<dd>
	<p>Any food for the household, such as:</p>

	<ul><li>Fruits and vegetables</li>
		<li>Meat, poultry, and fish</li>
		<li>Dairy products</li>
		<li>Breads and cereals</li>
		<li>Other foods such as snack foods and non-alcoholic beverages</li>
		<li>Seeds and plants which produce food for the household to eat</li>
	</ul><p>You CANNOT use SNAP benefits to buy:</p>

	<ul><li>Beer, wine, liquor, cigarettes, or tobacco</li>
		<li>Vitamins, medicines, and supplements. If an item has a Supplement Facts label, it is considered a supplement and it not eligible for SNAP purchase</li>
		<li>Live animals (except shellfish, fish removed from the water, and animals slaughtered prior to pick-up from the store)</li>
		<li>Foods that are hot at the point of sale</li>
		<li>Any nonfood items such as pet foods, cleaning supplies, paper products, and other household supplies</li>
	</ul></dd>
	<dt>Where can SNAP/SUN Bucks EBT benefits be used?</dt>
	<dd>
	<p><strong>In person at stores:</strong></p>

	<p>SNAP EBT benefits can be used at any participating retailer including convenience stores, farmers' markets and co-ops. Most stores that accept EBT will display a SNAP or Quest logo. You can also find a store near you that accepts SNAP by entering your zip code in the <a href="https://www.fns.usda.gov/snap/retailer-locator">USDA SNAP Retail Locator</a>.</p>

	<p><strong>Online:</strong></p>

	<p>You can also purchase eligible food items online at participating retailers using your EBT card.</p>

	<ul><li>SNAP benefits can be used online at participating retailers for delivery or curbside pick-up purchases. A list of participating retailers in Maine is available from the <a href="https://www.fns.usda.gov/snap/online">USDA SNAP Online Purchasing Pilot</a>. Some may accept EBT cash.</li>
		<li>SNAP benefits cannot be used for delivery charges.</li>
		<li>Contact your local grocery store to see if they chose curbside pick-up as an option. Any grocer is allowed to purchase wireless EBT equipment which makes curbside pick-up possible.</li>
	</ul></dd>
	<dt>How can an EBT card be protected when shopping online?</dt>
	<dd>
	<p>Only send your EBT information over a secure network.</p>
	Do not use public computers or WiFi such as:

	<ul><li>Libraries,</li>
		<li>Complimentary WiFi at restaurants, or</li>
		<li>Other shared WiFi such as at hotels</li>
	</ul>
	Secure your home WiFi by updating your router's password/key

	<p>More tips are available from the <a href="https://consumer.ftc.gov/media/79929">US Federal Trade Commission Online Shopping Security Tips</a>.</p>

	<ul><li>If you are using your cellphone and are not connected to a secure network, use your data plan.</li>
	</ul></dd>
	<dt>What should I do if my EBT benefits are stolen electronically?</dt>
	<dd>
	<ol><li>Have your card disabled and new one issued by calling 1-800- 477-7428.</li>
		<li>Change your PIN to one that is easy for you to remember but hard for others to guess.</li>
		<li><a href="https://reportfraud.ftc.gov/">You can file a report with the Federal Trade Commission</a> or connect with your local law enforcement.</li>
	</ol></dd>
</dl><h2>SNAP Work, ABAWD, and Student Rules</h2>

<dl class="ckeditor-accordion"><dt>What are the General Work Requirements and who do they apply to?</dt>
	<dd>
	<p>The SNAP General Work Requirements are if you are age 16 to 59 and able to work, you will likely need to meet these requirements in order to get SNAP benefits. The general work requirements include:</p>

	<ul><li>Respond to requests about employment status or availability to work from the Department, the Maine JobLink, and/or prospective employers.</li>
		<li>Not voluntarily quitting a job or reducing work hours below 30 a week without a good reason.</li>
	</ul></dd>
	<dt>Am I exempt from General Work Requirements?</dt>
	<dd>
	<p>You are excused from the general work requirements if you are any one of these things:</p>

	<ul><li>Working (including self-employed) at least 30 hours a week, or earning wages at least equal to the federal minimum wage multiplied by 30 hours ($217.50 per week);</li>
		<li>Meeting work requirements for another program (TANF or unemployment compensation);</li>
		<li>Taking care of a child under six or an incapacitated person;</li>
		<li>Unable to work due to a physical or mental limitation;</li>
		<li>Participating regularly in a substance use disorder treatment program;</li>
		<li>Studying in school or a training program at least half-time <a href="https://www.fns.usda.gov/snap/students">(but college students are subject to other eligibility rules)</a><a class="file file--mime-application-pdf file--application-pdf" data-entity-type="file" data-entity-uuid="b7dec65d-e099-4e7e-8b71-68b45d48ca89" filename="Community-College-Verification-Form Fillable 10.10.2.24_0.pdf" href="/dhhs/sites/maine.gov.dhhs/files/inline-files/Community-College-Verification-Form%20Fillable%2010.10.2.24_0.pdf" title="Community College Verification Fillable Form 10.10.24">.</a></li>
	</ul></dd>
	<dt>What happens if the General Work Requirements are not met?</dt>
	<dd>
	<p>If you have to meet the general work requirements but you don't, you are disqualified from getting SNAP for at least a month and must start meeting the requirements to get SNAP again. If you get back on SNAP and don't meet the requirements again, you are disqualified for longer than a month and you could be permanently disqualified. </p>
	</dd>
	<dt>What are SNAP's Able Bodied Adults Without Dependents (ABAWD) work requirements?</dt>
	<dd>
	<p>If you are 18-64, able to work, and do not live with a child under age 14, you might need to meet both the general work requirements and ABAWD requirements. You can meet the ABAWD work requirement by doing any one of these things:</p>

	<ul><li>Work at least 80 hours a month. Work can be for pay, for goods or services (for something other than money), or unpaid;</li>
		<li>Participate in a work program for at least 80 hours a month. A work program could be a qualifying component of SNAP Employment and Training (E&amp;T) or another federal, state, or local work program. Please note, SNAP E&amp;T supports are only available to individuals receiving SNAP;</li>
		<li>Participate in a combination of work and work program hours for a total of at least 80 hours a month;</li>
		<li>Participate in a workfare or volunteer community service for the value of the household benefit amount divided by the state minimum wage. A form is available <a href="https://www.maine.gov/dhhs/ofi/applications-forms">here</a> to help verify volunteer hours.</li>
	</ul></dd>
	<dt>How many months of SNAP can an ABAWD receive?</dt>
	<dd>
	<p>If they are meeting the requirements and eligible, SNAP eligibility is determined for 12 months (one year). If an ABAWD is not meeting requirements, they can get SNAP for only three months in the defined three year period (the time limit).</p>
	</dd>
	<dt>Are there exemptions from the ABAWD work requirement?</dt>
	<dd>
	<p>Someone is exempt from the ABAWD work requirement and time limit if you are any one of these things:</p>

	<ul><li>Unable to work due to a physical or mental limitation;</li>
		<li>Pregnant;</li>
		<li>Have someone under 14 in your SNAP household;</li>
		<li>Excused from the general work requirements (see the general work requirements questions);</li>
		<li>Member of a federally recognized Indigenous tribe or otherwise eligible for Indian Health Services through tribal affiliation.</li>
	</ul></dd>
	<dt>What happens if someone is not exempt and doesn't meet the ABAWD work requirement?</dt>
	<dd>
	<p>If a person who is an ABAWD is not meeting the requirements, they will lose SNAP benefits after three months. To get SNAP again, they must meet the ABAWD work requirement for a 30-day period or tell OFI about an exemption. Otherwise, you will need to wait until the end of your three-year period, when you'll get another three months of eligibility under the new 36 month time limit.</p>
	</dd>
	<dt>How exactly does the time limit work?</dt>
	<dd>
	<p>People who fall under ABAWD rules can receive SNAP benefits for a maximum of three months within a 36-month period, also known as the time limit. These months do not need to be consecutive. Once the SNAP months are exhausted, ABAWDs must meet the monthly work requirement to continue receiving benefits (unless exempt).</p>
	</dd>
	<dt>Where can I go for more ABAWD information?</dt>
	<dd>
	<p>In May 2026, we created a Q&amp;A document based on questions received from SNAP participants and our partners with more ABAWD information. Learn more at: <a href="https://acrobat.adobe.com/id/urn:aaid:sc:VA6C2:b79bbaea-8f09-4a41-8ee4-3104a970fe81">ABAWD Q&amp;A Resource - May 2026</a>.</p>
	</dd>
	<dt>I am a post secondary student. Am I potentially eligible for SNAP?</dt>
	<dd>
	<p>Generally, students attending an institution of higher education (i.e., college, university, trade/technical school) more than 'half-time' are only eligible for SNAP if they meet an exemption. The institution of higher education determines what is considered 'half-time' enrollment. Students who meet an exemption must also meet all other SNAP eligibility requirements.</p>
	</dd>
	<dt>What are the student exemptions to potentially qualify for SNAP?</dt>
	<dd>
	<p>If someone is a student and meets SNAP eligibility requirements, they may be eligible for SNAP if they meet the following exemptions:</p>

	<ul><li>Age 17 years old and under OR 50 years or older</li>
		<li>Physically or mentally unable to work 20 hours per week (disability or doctor's note)</li>
		<li>Responsible for the care of a household member under six years of age, even if childcare is available</li>
		<li>Responsible for the care of a household member between six to twelve years of age, if no childcare is available OR if the student is enrolled full-time and is the sole parent in the household</li>
		<li>Receiving TANF/PaS Enrolled at a community college in certain programs (Verified by completing this <a class="file file--mime-application-pdf file--application-pdf" data-entity-type="file" data-entity-uuid="d46628c4-afc5-41d6-a420-4c0507ccb3a5" filename="Community-College-Verification-Form Fillable 10.10.2.24_1.pdf" href="/dhhs/sites/maine.gov.dhhs/files/inline-files/Community-College-Verification-Form%20Fillable%2010.10.2.24_1.pdf" title="Community College Verification Fillable Form.pdf">Community College Verification Form (PDF)</a></li>
		<li>Participating in an on-the-job training program</li>
		<li>Placed in a training/education program by a government program like SNAP E&amp;T, WIOA, TAA, CSSP, HOPE, etc.</li>
		<li>Engaged in paid employment of 20 hours or more per week. If self-employed, be earning an average of 20 hours per week at the federal minimum wage of $7.25 (20 hours x $7.25 = $145 per week)</li>
	</ul></dd>
	<dt>Can someone still qualify for SNAP if they have a campus meal plan?</dt>
	<dd>
	<p>No. If someone lives on campus and receive the majority of their meals through either a mandatory or optional meal plan, they do not qualify for SNAP.</p>
	</dd>
</dl><p> </p>

<dl class="ckeditor-accordion"><dd>
	<ul></ul></dd>
</dl><h2>Information for Retailers</h2>

<p>Current SNAP-eligible retailers interested in participating in online EBT purchase should visit <a href="https://www.fns.usda.gov/snap/retailer/online/requirements">the USDA website</a> for more information, including program requirements and instruction for enrollment. </p>

<h2>SNAP Employment &amp; Training (E&amp;T) Program</h2>

<p>SNAP E&amp;T helps connect SNAP participants with job training and education. There are lots of opportunities for job readiness activities and support available at no cost. <a href="https://www.maine.gov/dhhs/ofi/programs-services/food-supplement/fset">Learn more about SNAP E&amp;T</a></p>

<p> </p>

<h2>SNAP Outreach</h2>

<p>SNAP outreach aims to increase awareness of the SNAP program and provide support to those applying for SNAP. OFI contracts with community-based organizations to offer SNAP outreach services across Maine.</p>

<p><a class="home_cta" data-entity-substitution="canonical" data-entity-type="node" data-entity-uuid="8081558e-80fe-48ec-94fe-bbee69c0250c" href="/dhhs/ofi/programs-services/food-supplement/snap-outreach">Learn More about SNAP Outreach</a></p>

<h2>SNAP Quality Control Review </h2>

<article data-once="quickedit" data-quickedit-entity-id="node/7565" data-quickedit-entity-instance-id="0"><p>Accurate SNAP determinations are critical to Maine's SNAP Program administration and taken seriously by Maine and our federal partners at the USDA and Food Nutrition Administration (FNA). The SNAP Quality Control (QC) Review Unit completes a thorough review of more than 1,100 randomly selected SNAP households every year. If a household is selected through this lottery, they must cooperate with their requests.</p>

<p><a href="https://www.maine.gov/dhhs/ofi/programs-services/food-supplement/QC">Learn more about SNAP Quality Control Review</a></p>
</article><h2>USDA Non Discrimination Statement</h2>

<p><a href="https://www.usda.gov/non-discrimination-statement">Read the USDA's Statement on Non discrimination statement.</a></p>

<p> </p>

      



